{
 "cells": [
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## DAF Project: Disorder in the 2D Anderson Model\r\n#### Author: André Faria 113318",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {},
   "executionSummary": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### 1. Introduction\r\n\r\nUnderstanding the effects of disorder on quantum systems is central to the study of condensed matter physics. The Anderson model is a useful tool for exploring how randomness can induce localization in non-interacting electrons.\r\n\r\nIn this study, we apply similar methods to those employed in lesson 7 to study disorder in a 100 by 100 2D Anderson model. These include the study of the Density of States (DoS), IPR as a function of energy and Level-Spacing Distribution (LSD) with varying disorder strengths while considering the hopping paramter $t$ a constant. We expect to see a prevalence of extended states for lower disorder values, while high disorder values overpower electron hopping and in turn localise each state.\r\n\r\n### 2. Fundamental Theory\r\n\r\n#### Localization\r\n\r\nLocalization refers to the phenomenon where a particle's quantum wavefunction becomes confined due to disorder in the system. In a clean system, an electron's wavefunction can spread out over the entire lattice. It's extended, which allows for conduction.\r\n\r\nWhen disorder is introduced, interference effects can cause the wavefunction to become localised in space, no longer contributing to conduction. This means the probability of finding the electron drops exponentially as you move away from the central point.\r\n\r\n#### Two dimensional time-independent Schrodinger equation with a disorded potencial\r\n\r\nJust like for the one-dimensional model studied briefly in lesson 7, a continuous two-dimensional model of a particle is considered, in a disorded potencial $V(x)$. By discreting the corresponding time-independent Schrodinger equation on a lattice with sufficently small spacing, an approximation of the 2D Anderson model can be obtained. The equation in question is given by:\r\n\r\n$$\r\n-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m_0} \\left( \\frac{\\partial^2 \\psi}{\\partial x^2} + \\frac{\\partial^2 \\psi}{\\partial y^2} \\right) + V(x, y)\\psi = E \\psi\r\n$$\r\n\r\nwhere $V(x,y)$ is some random potential and $E$ is the eigenenergy.\r\n\r\n#### Discretization\r\n\r\nDiscretizing the equation for small but finite differences, $\\Delta x = \\Delta y = a$, allows the second order partial derivatives to be expressed as\r\n\r\n$$\r\n\\frac{\\partial^2 \\psi}{\\partial x^2} \\approx \\frac{\\psi_{i+1,j} + \\psi_{i-1,j} - 2\\psi_{i,j}}{a^2} \\quad \\quad \\frac{\\partial^2 \\psi}{\\partial y^2} \\approx \\frac{\\psi_{i,j+1} + \\psi_{i,j-1} - 2\\psi_{i,j}}{a^2}\r\n$$\r\n\r\nThe $\\frac{\\partial^2\\psi}{\\partial x^2}$ and $\\frac{\\partial^2\\psi}{\\partial y^2}$ operatores can be expressed as a tridiagonal matrix\r\n\r\n$$\r\nH_x = \\begin{bmatrix}\r\n-2 & 1 & 0 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n1 & -2 & 1 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n0 & 1 & -2 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n\\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & 1 \\\\\r\n0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\r\n\\end{bmatrix}_{L \\times L}\r\n$$\r\n\r\n$$\r\nH_y = \\begin{bmatrix}\r\n-2 & 1 & 0 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n1 & -2 & 1 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n0 & 1 & -2 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n\\vdots & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & 1 \\\\\r\n0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\r\n\\end{bmatrix}_{L \\times L}\r\n$$\r\nwhile the potencial operator is a diagonal matrix\r\n\r\n$$\r\nV = \r\n\\begin{bmatrix}\r\nV_{1,1} & 0 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n0 & V_{1,2} & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\\r\n0 & 0 & \\cdots & V_{L,L}\r\n\\end{bmatrix}\r\n$$\r\n\r\nso the equation in matrix form is\r\n\r\n$$\r\n\\begin{bmatrix}\r\n4a_0 + V_{1,1} & -a_0 & 0 & \\cdots & -a_0 & 0 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n-a_0 & 4a_0 + V_{1,2} & -a_0 & \\cdots & 0 & -a_0 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n0 & -a_0 & \\ddots & \\ddots & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\\r\n\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\ddots & -a_0 & 0 & \\cdots & -a_0 \\\\\r\n-a_0 & 0 & \\cdots & -a_0 & 4a_0 + V_{2,1} & -a_0 & \\cdots & 0 \\\\\r\n0 & -a_0 & \\cdots & 0 & -a_0 & 4a_0 + V_{2,2} & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\\r\n\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\ddots & -a_0 \\\\\r\n0 & 0 & \\cdots & -a_0 & 0 & \\cdots & -a_0 & 4a_0 + V_{L,L}\r\n\\end{bmatrix}\r\n\\begin{bmatrix}\r\n\\Psi_{1,1} \\\\\r\n\\Psi_{1,2} \\\\\r\n\\vdots \\\\\r\n\\Psi_{1,L} \\\\\r\n\\Psi_{2,1} \\\\\r\n\\vdots \\\\\r\n\\Psi_{L,L}\r\n\\end{bmatrix}\r\n= E\r\n\\begin{bmatrix}\r\n\\Psi_{1,1} \\\\\r\n\\Psi_{1,2} \\\\\r\n\\vdots \\\\\r\n\\Psi_{1,L} \\\\\r\n\\Psi_{2,1} \\\\\r\n\\vdots \\\\\r\n\\Psi_{L,L}\r\n\\end{bmatrix}\r\n$$\r\n\r\nwhere $a_0 = \\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m a^2}$ is a kinetic energy contant.\r\n\r\nThe previous discretization motivates the Anderson model, where the 2D Hamiltonian is given by:\r\n\r\n$$\r\nH = \\sum_{i,j} \\left( w_{i,j} |i,j\\rangle \\langle i,j| \r\n+ t_x |i,j\\rangle \\langle i+1,j| \r\n+ t_y |i,j\\rangle \\langle i,j+1| \r\n+ \\text{h.c.} \\right)\r\n$$\r\n\r\nwhere the state $|i,j\\rangle$ is the occupation amplitude of the site $(i,j)$, with its on-site energy $w_\\text{i,j} = V_{i,j}$, and $t \\equiv a_0$ is the tunneling matrix element coupling neighbour sites.\r\n\r\nDisorder is introduced into the system by allowing the on-site energies $w_\\text{i,j}$ to random variables, uniformly distributed in the interval $[\\frac{-W}{2}, \\frac{W}{2}]$, with $W$ measuring the disorder strength.\r\n\r\n#### Density of states\r\n\r\nThe DoS tells us how many quantum states are available at each energy level for particles like electrons, in the case of this study. For the 2D Anderson model with hopping $t = 1$ the disorder free bandwidth is about $[-4, 4]$, which can be broadend by a random on-site disorder $W$.\r\n\r\n#### The Inverse Participation Ratio\r\n\r\nIn order to decide whether or not a state is localized it's often sufficent to consider the second moment of the probability density,\r\n\r\n$$\r\n\\text{IPR} = P^{-1} = \\sum_{i,j} |\\psi_\\text{i,j}|^4 \\approx \r\n\\begin{cases}\r\n1/N  \\quad \\quad(extended)\\\\\r\nConst. \\quad (localized)\r\n\\end{cases}\r\n$$\r\n\r\nwhere $N$ is the number of lattice sites. \r\n\r\nThis is the inverse participation number. It's a measure of the portion of the space where the amplitude of the wavefunction differs from zero. IPR vs energy plots reveal critical energies where the states transition from extended to localized.\r\n\r\n#### Level-Spacing Distribution\r\n\r\nAs the name implies, the Level-Spacing Distribution shows you the distribution of level spacings, which are the differences between consecutive eigenenergies.\r\n\r\nFor a 2D system of size $L \\times L$, there are $N = L^2$ eigenenergies. The level-spacing distributions statistics can distinguish between two distributions. \r\nFor low disorder values ($W \\lesssim 2$) the LSD is expected to resemble a Wigner-Dyson distribution, which is associated with a largely still extended system. For higher disorder values ($W \\gtrsim 10$) instead resembles a Poisson distribution, associated with a largely localised system.\r\nIn between is a transition period where the LSD changes its behaviour, where there is a mix of localized and extended states.\r\n\r\n### 3. Code\r\n",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {},
   "executionSummary": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "ModelAnalysis2D[nx_, ny_, W_] := Module[\r\n    {\r\n        w, hamiltonian, eigenValues, eigenVectors, IPR, levelSpacings,\r\n        posToIndex, indexToPos, totalSites = nx*ny,\r\n        binWidth = 0.1, bins, binCenters, dosData\r\n    },\r\n\r\n    (* Coordinate ↔ index helpers *)\r\n    posToIndex[{x_, y_}] := (y - 1)*nx + x;\r\n    indexToPos[index_] := {Mod[index - 1, nx] + 1, Quotient[index - 1, nx] + 1};\r\n\r\n    (* Generate disorder potential *)\r\n    w = RandomReal[{-W/2, W/2}, totalSites];\r\n\r\n    (* Full dense Hamiltonian *)\r\n    hamiltonian = ConstantArray[0., {totalSites, totalSites}];\r\n    Do[\r\n        hamiltonian[[i, i]] = w[[i]],\r\n        {i, 1, totalSites}\r\n    ];\r\n\r\n    (* Nearest-neighbor hopping *)\r\n    Do[\r\n        {x, y} = indexToPos[i];\r\n        (* Right neighbour hoping *)\r\n        If[x < nx,\r\n            j = posToIndex[{x + 1, y}];\r\n            hamiltonian[[i, j]] = -1;   (* t = 1 implied by -1 *)\r\n            hamiltonian[[j, i]] = -1;\r\n        ];\r\n        (* Left neighbour hoping *)\r\n        If[y < ny,\r\n            j = posToIndex[{x, y + 1}];\r\n            hamiltonian[[i, j]] = -1;   (* t = 1 implied by -1 *)\r\n            hamiltonian[[j, i]] = -1;\r\n        ];\r\n        ,\r\n        {i, 1, totalSites}\r\n    ];\r\n\r\n    (* Diagonalise Hamiltonian *)\r\n    {eigenValues, eigenVectors} = Eigensystem[hamiltonian];\r\n\r\n    (* IPR (vectors are orthonormal already) *)\r\n    IPR = Total[#^4] & /@ eigenVectors;\r\n\r\n    (* Level spacings *)\r\n    levelSpacings = Differences[Sort[eigenValues]];\r\n\r\n    (* Density of States (scatter style) *)\r\n    bins = BinCounts[eigenValues, {Min[eigenValues], Max[eigenValues], binWidth}];\r\n    binCenters = Range[\r\n        Min[eigenValues] + binWidth/2,\r\n        Max[eigenValues] - binWidth/2,\r\n        binWidth\r\n    ];\r\n    dosData = Transpose[{binCenters, bins}];\r\n\r\n    (* Return output plots *)\r\n    <|\r\n        \"DOS\" -> ListPlot[\r\n            dosData,\r\n            PlotStyle -> PointSize[Medium],\r\n            PlotLabel -> StringForm[\"Density of States (W = `1`)\", W],\r\n            AxesLabel -> {\"Energy\", \"Density\"},\r\n            ImageSize -> Medium\r\n        ],\r\n\r\n        \"IPR\" -> ListPlot[\r\n            Transpose[{eigenValues, IPR}],\r\n            PlotLabel -> StringForm[\"IPR vs Energy (W = `1`)\", W],\r\n            AxesLabel -> {\"Energy\", \"IPR\"},\r\n            PlotRange -> All,\r\n            ImageSize -> Medium\r\n        ],\r\n\r\n        \"LevelSpacing\" -> Histogram[\r\n            levelSpacings,\r\n            Automatic,\r\n            \"PDF\",\r\n            ChartStyle -> RGBColor[0.3, 0.6, 0.9],\r\n            PlotLabel -> StringForm[\"Level Spacing Distribution (W = `1`)\", W],\r\n            AxesLabel -> {\"Level Spacing\", \"Frequency\"},\r\n            ImageSize -> Medium\r\n        ]\r\n    |>\r\n]\r\n\r\n\r\n(* Parameters *)\r\nnx = 100; (* Number of sites in x-direction *)\r\nny = 100; (* Number of sites in y-direction *)\r\nt = 1; (* hopping paramter, t_x = t_y *)\r\n\r\n(* Aumentar bins para w maior *)",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {},
   "executionSummary": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* No disorder -> W = 0 *)\r\nW = 0;\r\n\r\nanalysis2D = ModelAnalysis2D[nx, ny, W];\r\n\r\nGraphicsRow[{\r\n    analysis2D[\"DOS\"],\r\n    analysis2D[\"IPR\"]\r\n}, ImageSize -> Large]\r\n\r\nanalysis2D[\"LevelSpacing\"]",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
